              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Interpreting a pregnancy test can sometimes feel confusing or stressful, especially if the result isn't a bold, clear line. Home pregnancy tests work by detecting **hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin)**, a hormone produced by the developing placenta shortly after an embryo implants in the uterus. \n\nUnderstanding how to read your test, what a faint line means, and how to ensure the most accurate results will help clarify your reading.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Golden Rule: Read Within the Time Window\nEvery pregnancy test has a strict instructions packet that dictates a reading window\u2014usually **3 to 5 minutes**. \n* **Do not read a test after 10 minutes**. As the urine on the test strip dries, it can create a faint, colorless line (called an **evaporation line**) that can easily be mistaken for a positive result. \n\n### 2. Control Line vs. Test Line\nStandard (non-digital) line tests have two distinct areas:\n* **The Control Line (C):** This line *must* appear. It proves that the test strip absorbed enough liquid and is functioning properly. If no control line appears, the test is **invalid**, and you must throw it out and try a new one.\n* **The Test Line (T):** This line only appears if hCG is detected in your urine. \n\n---\n\n### 3. Interpreting Your Results\n\n| Result Type | What It Looks Like | What It Means |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Clear Positive** | Two distinct, colored lines (or a \"+\" sign / \"Pregnant\" on a digital test) within the time window. | **You are pregnant.** Even if one line is slightly lighter than the control, if it has distinct color (pink or blue), it is a positive result. |\n| **Faint Positive** | A very faint, but distinctly **colored** (pink or blue) line that appears within the 3-5 minute window. | **Likely positive.** This usually indicates very early pregnancy when hCG levels are still low. Your hCG levels double roughly every 48 hours in early pregnancy. |\n| **Evaporation Line** | A faint, **colorless, gray, or shadow-like** line that appears *after* the test has dried (usually past the 10-minute mark). | **Negative.** This is a chemical reaction from drying urine, not a positive result. |\n| **Negative** | Only the control line is visible; the test area remains completely blank within the reading window. | **Not pregnant, or tested too early.** If you tested before your missed period, your hCG levels may not be high enough to detect yet. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Tips for the Most Accurate Results\n* **Test with first morning urine:** Your urine is most concentrated when you first wake up. If you test later in the day, highly diluted urine (from drinking water) can cause a false negative or an incredibly faint line.\n* **Wait for your missed period:** While \"early detection\" tests can find hCG a few days before your period is due, testing on or after the first day of your missed period provides over 99% accuracy. \n* **Prefer pink-dye or digital tests:** If you are struggling to read faint lines, pink-dye tests are generally easier to read than blue-dye tests (which are highly prone to confusing evaporation lines). Digital tests remove all guesswork by explicitly reading \"Pregnant\" or \"Not Pregnant\".\n\n---\n\n### What to Do Next\n\n* **If your test is positive (even faint):** Contact your OB/GYN or primary care provider to schedule a blood test and your first prenatal appointment. If you haven't already, start taking a daily prenatal vitamin containing at least 400 mcg of folic acid.\n* **If your test is negative but your period is late:** Wait 48 hours and test again using your first morning urine. If you continue to miss your period and get negative results, contact your doctor to investigate other reasons for a delayed cycle.\n\n*Because different brands have unique layouts (such as vertical/horizontal lines, plus signs, or digital screens), always double-check the specific instruction pamphlet included in your test box.*",
